THOUGHT: NullAway complains that the default constructor doesn’t initialize non-null fields `transparentcolor` and `absolutePath`. They’re used in `equals`, `hashCode`, and `toString` without null checks, so they should be non-null. I’ll initialize them in the no-arg constructor with sensible defaults (e.g., `Color(0,0,0,0)` and a dummy URL like `new URL("file:")`), which guarantees non-null values on all paths and avoids changing other logic. Then I’ll run the Gradle compile to confirm the NullAway error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/litiengine && sed -i '31,33c\  public MapImage() {
    super();
    this.transparentcolor = new Color(0, 0, 0, 0);
    try {
      this.absolutePath = new URL("file:");
    } catch (MalformedURLException e) {
      throw new RuntimeException(e);
    }
  }' src/de/gurkenlabs/litiengine/environment/tilemap/xml/MapImage.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1
```

